{"chapter": "groupoids-quotients", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "048M", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:2d86ee44b67c4ca5ad8e855f2a695885e8ef58959cc7b7f8ce73a9a526a1ab75", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nBecause we have seen above that the notion of a set-theoretically\ninvariant morphism is a rather weak one in the category of algebraic\nspaces, we define an orbit space for a pre-relation as follows.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fa4aeff2d37bc2b3af5458d91533d70263bd99f989fa1e2271fefcc76549a9a4", "unit_id": "tag:048M:prose-0010"}
{"chapter": "groupoids-quotients", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "048M", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$B \\to S$", "MATH_0002": "$j : R \\to U \\times_B U$", "MATH_0003": "$\\phi : U \\to X$", "MATH_0004": "$R$", "MATH_0005": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0006": "$R$", "MATH_0007": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0008": "$R$", "MATH_0009": "$\\phi$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{section-conventions-notation}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\end{enumerate}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-orbit-space}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:f7b0fb94fa1d93f4d63a8e205e696625e488b23cc542fdee5dd60677e18e2988", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:45e390652b990527ed16a6da78c6d49c6007f65cde7cfd6fbdb0f7d4688db161", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> as in Section <REF_0001>.\nLet <MATH_0002> be a pre-relation.\nWe say <MATH_0003> is an <TEXTITOPEN_0001>orbit space for <MATH_0004><TEXTITCLOSE_0001> if\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> <MATH_0005> is <MATH_0006>-invariant,\n<STRUCT_0003> <MATH_0007> separates <MATH_0008>-orbits, and\n<STRUCT_0004> <MATH_0009> is surjective.\n<STRUCT_0005>\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b234794f8c6415b080760c36578ffae8d2b8158e29260ef7efbb7d1464246778", "unit_id": "tag:0493:statement"}
{"chapter": "groupoids-quotients", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "048M", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$R$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:e5fc7892ed5d3c89859f320312560ac7543d92ccd56c8b74f4e03c4acf0d532c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:822188ddeb7e0d1ff5671efbc13c98ea934515764373cd6633530b840a1d567a", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe definition of separating <MATH_0001>-orbits involves a discussion of\npoints with values in algebraically closed fields. But as we've seen\nin many cases this just corresponds to the surjectivity of certain\ncanonically associated morphisms of algebraic spaces.\nWe summarize some of the discussion above in the following characterization\nof orbit spaces.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:a6d953b963941ddb8b24d60e9812059783853a00bd1c81836e85e9a6c92ceece", "unit_id": "tag:048M:prose-0011"}
{"chapter": "groupoids-quotients", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "048M", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$B \\to S$", "MATH_0002": "$j : R \\to U \\times_B U$", "MATH_0003": "$\\phi : U \\to X$", "MATH_0004": "$R$", "MATH_0005": "$\\phi \\circ s = \\phi \\circ t$", "MATH_0006": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0007": "$(t, s) : R \\to U \\times_X U$", "MATH_0008": "$\\phi : U \\to X$", "MATH_0009": "$j$", "MATH_0010": "$B$", "MATH_0011": "$B$", "MATH_0012": "$U$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{section-conventions-notation}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\end{enumerate}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-orbit-space}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:2b9790cc914aff33a26dabb83aa375ebce2251da48b4670cd4dfa23f8a108e37", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8c7305bb5c5c6499a35c1ae86e36eeba3175c8aa6d52348714900fa116a4a8d5", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> as in Section <REF_0001>.\nLet <MATH_0002> be a set-theoretic pre-equivalence\nrelation. A morphism <MATH_0003> is an orbit space for <MATH_0004> if and only if\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> <MATH_0005>, i.e., <MATH_0006> is invariant,\n<STRUCT_0003> the induced morphism <MATH_0007> is surjective, and\n<STRUCT_0004> the morphism <MATH_0008> is surjective.\n<STRUCT_0005>\nThis characterization applies for example if <MATH_0009> is a pre-equivalence relation,\nor comes from a groupoid in algebraic spaces over <MATH_0010>, or comes from the action\nof a group algebraic space over <MATH_0011> on <MATH_0012>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e51fd7b1b8c2f93fc53f005ec338217f86dd539ad569bd9821f9b31b5727e197", "unit_id": "tag:049Z:statement"}
